// **the payoff case**: one file, three platforms, and only one of them is ever parsed.
//
// each arm declares the *same* function, which is what makes this more than a formatting exercise - as
// three ordinary declarations they would collide as a DuplicateFunctionSignature. Nothing collides because
// the two that do not apply never reach pass 2 at all, and that is the whole point of the filter running
// before the parser rather than inside it.
//
// asserted from every side by the `.test` files beside this one: `--target-os` picks an arm without
// touching codegen, so all three are checked on one machine.

#[if: os == darwin]
function platform() : string { return 'darwin'; }
#[elif: os == linux]
function platform() : string { return 'linux'; }
#[elif: os == windows]
function platform() : string { return 'windows'; }
#[end]

echo platform();
